1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:02,540
Look at a clock on one side of

2
00:00:02,390 --> 00:00:05,020
a room, and a second clock sitting on

3
00:00:04,870 --> 00:00:06,550
the opposite wall.

4
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:09,930
If both read three o'clock, you naturally assume

5
00:00:09,780 --> 00:00:12,230
they are showing the same now.

6
00:00:12,080 --> 00:00:15,870
But stop and ask a surprisingly simple question:

7
00:00:15,720 --> 00:00:18,950
how do you actually know they agree?

8
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:21,670
You might say, just look at both clocks.

9
00:00:21,520 --> 00:00:24,790
The trouble is, light takes time to cross

10
00:00:24,640 --> 00:00:25,510
the room.

11
00:00:25,360 --> 00:00:27,830
So the image arriving from the far wall

12
00:00:27,690 --> 00:00:31,700
is already slightly old which means to trust

13
00:00:31,550 --> 00:00:34,150
that clock, you first have to subtract the

14
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:37,870
time light spent traveling from there to your

15
00:00:37,720 --> 00:00:37,870
eyes.

16
00:00:37,720 --> 00:00:40,740
And to calculate that travel time, you need

17
00:00:40,590 --> 00:00:42,950
to know the speed of light in one

18
00:00:42,800 --> 00:00:44,020
direction.

19
00:00:43,880 --> 00:00:46,910
Here is the shock that stopped Albert Einstein

20
00:00:46,760 --> 00:00:51,110
in his tracks: no experiment in human history

21
00:00:50,960 --> 00:00:54,470
has ever measured the one-way speed of light.

22
00:00:54,360 --> 00:00:56,880
Every measurement we have ever made is a

23
00:00:56,730 --> 00:00:58,940
two-way round trip.

24
00:00:58,840 --> 00:01:01,890
And because of that single blind spot, there

25
00:01:01,740 --> 00:01:03,690
is no natural way to say what is

26
00:01:03,540 --> 00:01:07,360
happening right now anywhere else in the universe.

27
00:01:08,074 --> 00:01:11,034
Wait why can't we just measure light in

28
00:01:10,884 --> 00:01:13,384
one direction right now?

29
00:01:13,459 --> 00:01:14,574
Let's try it.

30
00:01:14,424 --> 00:01:16,624
Put Clock A on the left side of

31
00:01:16,474 --> 00:01:19,554
a long track, and Clock B one kilometer

32
00:01:19,584 --> 00:01:21,254
away on the right.

33
00:01:21,144 --> 00:01:23,274
Fire a laser pulse from A when it

34
00:01:23,124 --> 00:01:26,234
reads zero, record the exact time it hits

35
00:01:26,084 --> 00:01:28,894
Clock B, and divide distance by time.

36
00:01:28,784 --> 00:01:30,684
Done right?

37
00:01:30,624 --> 00:01:32,314
Not so fast.

38
00:01:32,184 --> 00:01:35,104
That stopwatch only works if Clock A and

39
00:01:34,954 --> 00:01:38,094
Clock B were already synchronized before you fired

40
00:01:37,944 --> 00:01:39,114
the laser.

41
00:01:38,984 --> 00:01:41,654
If Clock B was sitting even a microsecond

42
00:01:42,044 --> 00:01:45,234
fast or slow, your speed measurement is completely

43
00:01:45,084 --> 00:01:46,214
wrong.

44
00:01:46,309 --> 00:01:50,054
Okay, fine so let's synchronize the two clocks

45
00:01:49,904 --> 00:01:50,294
first.

46
00:01:50,184 --> 00:01:51,414
How?

47
00:01:51,264 --> 00:01:53,554
If you send a radio or light signal

48
00:01:53,404 --> 00:01:56,224
from Clock A to set Clock B, you

49
00:01:56,074 --> 00:01:58,104
have to tell Clock B how long the

50
00:01:57,954 --> 00:01:59,914
signal spent in transit.

51
00:01:59,784 --> 00:02:01,654
Which means you already need to know the

52
00:02:01,504 --> 00:02:03,714
one-way speed of light!

53
00:02:03,624 --> 00:02:06,034
You are trapped in a circle: you need

54
00:02:05,884 --> 00:02:08,954
synchronized clocks to measure one-way light speed, and

55
00:02:08,804 --> 00:02:11,754
you need one-way light speed to synchronize the

56
00:02:11,604 --> 00:02:12,444
clocks.

57
00:02:12,384 --> 00:02:15,254
So maybe you try a clever workaround.

58
00:02:15,104 --> 00:02:16,934
Forget signals completely.

59
00:02:16,784 --> 00:02:19,244
Put both clocks right next to each other,

60
00:02:19,269 --> 00:02:21,914
sync them face to face, and then gently

61
00:02:21,764 --> 00:02:24,154
carry Clock B across the room.

62
00:02:24,024 --> 00:02:28,914
Sounds foolproof until Einstein's relativity steps in.

63
00:02:28,784 --> 00:02:31,294
The moment you move Clock B, time slows

64
00:02:31,144 --> 00:02:33,204
down for the moving clock.

65
00:02:33,104 --> 00:02:35,914
And here is the twist: if light really

66
00:02:35,764 --> 00:02:39,034
does travel at different speeds in different directions,

67
00:02:38,914 --> 00:02:42,974
then time dilation itself becomes direction-dependent.

68
00:02:42,824 --> 00:02:44,784
Even if you carry Clock B at a

69
00:02:44,634 --> 00:02:48,374
snail's pace, that tiny directional slowdown builds up

70
00:02:48,224 --> 00:02:50,904
across the whole trip shifting Clock B by

71
00:02:50,754 --> 00:02:54,034
the exact amount needed to hide any difference

72
00:02:53,884 --> 00:02:55,294
in light's speed.

73
00:02:55,144 --> 00:02:58,814
Every trick we try cables, spinning gears, moving

74
00:02:58,664 --> 00:03:01,834
clocks closes back into the exact same trap.

75
00:03:02,560 --> 00:03:05,730
So how did physicists ever measure the speed

76
00:03:05,580 --> 00:03:10,120
of light at three hundred thousand kilometers per

77
00:03:09,970 --> 00:03:10,130
second?

78
00:03:09,980 --> 00:03:12,500
By cheating with a mirror.

79
00:03:12,380 --> 00:03:15,130
Instead of two clocks, you use just one

80
00:03:14,980 --> 00:03:18,190
clock on Earth and place a mirror far

81
00:03:18,040 --> 00:03:18,480
away.

82
00:03:18,340 --> 00:03:21,020
You fire a laser pulse, bounce it off

83
00:03:20,870 --> 00:03:23,440
the mirror, and catch it right back at

84
00:03:23,290 --> 00:03:24,690
the same clock.

85
00:03:24,580 --> 00:03:26,730
No second clock to synchronize.

86
00:03:26,580 --> 00:03:29,720
Just one departure time, one return time, and

87
00:03:29,570 --> 00:03:31,820
the round-trip distance.

88
00:03:31,700 --> 00:03:35,200
That gives you the average round-trip speed the

89
00:03:35,050 --> 00:03:36,690
two-way speed of light.

90
00:03:36,540 --> 00:03:40,700
And every precision measurement ever performed, from nineteenth-century

91
00:03:40,550 --> 00:03:44,520
spinning mirrors to modern laser laboratories, measures this

92
00:03:44,370 --> 00:03:45,520
round trip.

93
00:03:45,380 --> 00:03:48,270
Now watch what that leaves wide open.

94
00:03:48,140 --> 00:03:51,940
Imagine an astronaut standing on Mars, ten light-minutes

95
00:03:51,790 --> 00:03:53,020
away from Earth.

96
00:03:52,900 --> 00:03:55,490
At exactly twelve o'clock, you beam a message

97
00:03:55,340 --> 00:03:58,060
out to Mars and wait for a reply.

98
00:03:57,940 --> 00:04:00,910
In the universe we all picture, light travels

99
00:04:00,760 --> 00:04:03,550
at full light speed going out taking ten

100
00:04:03,400 --> 00:04:06,540
minutes and full light speed coming back taking

101
00:04:06,390 --> 00:04:07,900
another ten minutes.

102
00:04:07,780 --> 00:04:10,690
The Martian astronaut gets your message at twelve-ten,

103
00:04:10,540 --> 00:04:13,640
replies immediately, and you receive their answer at

104
00:04:13,867 --> 00:04:14,768
twelve-twenty.

105
00:04:14,620 --> 00:04:17,880
Now imagine a wildly different universe.

106
00:04:17,740 --> 00:04:20,090
What if light travels at half the speed

107
00:04:19,940 --> 00:04:22,690
of light going toward Mars taking the full

108
00:04:23,050 --> 00:04:26,260
twenty minutes and then travels infinitely fast coming

109
00:04:26,110 --> 00:04:28,660
back, arriving in zero seconds?

110
00:04:28,540 --> 00:04:31,220
Wait instantaneous?!

111
00:04:31,300 --> 00:04:32,910
Let's check the clocks!

112
00:04:32,780 --> 00:04:34,810
You send your message at twelve o'clock.

113
00:04:34,660 --> 00:04:37,250
It crawls out to Mars for twenty minutes,

114
00:04:37,100 --> 00:04:40,450
arriving when Earth's clock reads twelve-twenty.

115
00:04:40,300 --> 00:04:43,420
The astronaut replies immediately, and the signal teleports

116
00:04:43,750 --> 00:04:47,410
back in zero seconds hitting Earth at twelve-twenty!

117
00:04:47,260 --> 00:04:50,520
Even worse, the astronaut can't catch the mismatch

118
00:04:50,370 --> 00:04:52,770
either, because the sync pulse you sent earlier

119
00:04:52,620 --> 00:04:55,130
to set their Mars clock also took twenty

120
00:04:54,980 --> 00:04:58,630
minutes to arrive quietly setting Mars ten minutes

121
00:04:58,480 --> 00:04:59,700
behind Earth.

122
00:04:59,580 --> 00:05:03,440
Every timestamp, every screen, every radio ping looks

123
00:05:03,502 --> 00:05:06,630
one hundred percent identical in both universes!

124
00:05:06,500 --> 00:05:09,400
If that just broke your intuition about how

125
00:05:09,250 --> 00:05:13,020
light works, hit the like button because Einstein's

126
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:14,770
solution is even stranger.

127
00:05:15,716 --> 00:05:18,626
In nineteen oh five, when Albert Einstein sat

128
00:05:18,476 --> 00:05:20,936
down to write his landmark paper on special

129
00:05:20,786 --> 00:05:24,876
relativity, he hit this exact wall on page

130
00:05:24,726 --> 00:05:24,946
two.

131
00:05:25,086 --> 00:05:27,966
Most people assume Einstein proved that light moves

132
00:05:27,816 --> 00:05:29,986
at the same speed in every direction.

133
00:05:29,906 --> 00:05:31,006
He didn't.

134
00:05:31,226 --> 00:05:34,176
Read his original words carefully: he wrote that

135
00:05:34,026 --> 00:05:37,376
equal outbound and inbound light travel time is

136
00:05:37,226 --> 00:05:39,546
not a physical fact we can prove about

137
00:05:39,396 --> 00:05:42,306
nature it is a rule we choose by

138
00:05:42,156 --> 00:05:43,536
definition.

139
00:05:43,546 --> 00:05:45,396
Here is Einstein's rule.

140
00:05:45,516 --> 00:05:47,546
Send a light pulse from Clock A to

141
00:05:47,396 --> 00:05:49,566
Clock B and bounce it right back.

142
00:05:49,666 --> 00:05:52,796
Whatever time Clock A records when the flash

143
00:05:52,646 --> 00:05:55,056
returns, simply declare that the pulse reached Clock

144
00:05:54,906 --> 00:05:56,916
B at the exact halfway mark.

145
00:05:57,066 --> 00:06:00,546
Later, the philosopher of physics Hans Reichenbach showed

146
00:06:00,396 --> 00:06:02,656
this is just one setting on a continuous

147
00:06:02,506 --> 00:06:03,686
dial.

148
00:06:03,746 --> 00:06:05,676
Set the dial to one half, and you

149
00:06:05,626 --> 00:06:09,486
get Einstein's clean, symmetrical universe where light moves

150
00:06:09,336 --> 00:06:11,336
at the same speed both ways.

151
00:06:11,406 --> 00:06:14,046
Turn that dial toward one, and light slows

152
00:06:13,896 --> 00:06:16,576
down one way while speeding up the other

153
00:06:16,546 --> 00:06:20,046
yet every physical prediction stays completely unchanged.

154
00:06:20,026 --> 00:06:23,906
That means simultaneous across two separate places isn't

155
00:06:23,756 --> 00:06:27,356
something hidden inside nature waiting to be discovered.

156
00:06:27,346 --> 00:06:29,866
It is a convention a rule you have

157
00:06:29,716 --> 00:06:32,136
to pick before the word now across a

158
00:06:31,986 --> 00:06:33,786
room even has a meaning.

159
00:06:33,786 --> 00:06:37,336
Okay so let's pick Einstein's clean halfway rule

160
00:06:37,186 --> 00:06:38,636
and stick with it.

161
00:06:38,546 --> 00:06:41,426
Surely now everyone can agree on what happens

162
00:06:41,276 --> 00:06:42,596
at the same time right?

163
00:06:43,485 --> 00:06:45,735
Not even close.

164
00:06:45,935 --> 00:06:49,485
Because the moment someone starts moving, Einstein's own

165
00:06:49,335 --> 00:06:53,065
rule forces two observers to disagree about what

166
00:06:52,915 --> 00:06:54,655
is happening right now.

167
00:06:54,735 --> 00:06:59,175
Picture a glass train speeding smoothly past a

168
00:06:59,025 --> 00:07:00,295
station platform.

169
00:07:00,355 --> 00:07:03,125
At the exact moment the passenger in the

170
00:07:02,975 --> 00:07:06,145
middle of the train passes an observer standing

171
00:07:05,995 --> 00:07:08,955
on the platform, two bolts of lightning strike

172
00:07:08,805 --> 00:07:11,005
the front and rear ends of the train.

173
00:07:11,115 --> 00:07:14,315
For the observer standing still on the platform,

174
00:07:14,685 --> 00:07:17,125
both bolts hit at the exact same distance

175
00:07:16,975 --> 00:07:17,435
away.

176
00:07:18,065 --> 00:07:20,685
Because light travels at the same speed from

177
00:07:20,535 --> 00:07:24,345
both ends, both flashes reach the platform observer

178
00:07:24,195 --> 00:07:24,805
together.

179
00:07:25,345 --> 00:07:29,055
Using Einstein's halfway rule, they conclude: the two

180
00:07:28,905 --> 00:07:31,765
strikes were completely simultaneous.

181
00:07:32,015 --> 00:07:34,955
Now step inside the moving train.

182
00:07:35,075 --> 00:07:39,205
The passenger uses the exact same rule light

183
00:07:39,055 --> 00:07:41,605
travels at the same speed from both ends

184
00:07:41,455 --> 00:07:42,045
of the car.

185
00:07:42,185 --> 00:07:45,095
Yet while those flashes are in midair, the

186
00:07:44,945 --> 00:07:48,135
passenger is rushing toward the light coming from

187
00:07:47,985 --> 00:07:50,405
the front, and running away from the light

188
00:07:50,255 --> 00:07:51,995
coming from the back.

189
00:07:51,945 --> 00:07:55,125
So the front flash hits their eyes first,

190
00:07:54,975 --> 00:07:57,675
and the rear flash arrives moments later.

191
00:07:57,675 --> 00:08:00,195
Since both ends of the train are equally

192
00:08:00,045 --> 00:08:03,285
far from their seat, the passenger must conclude

193
00:08:03,135 --> 00:08:07,205
something totally different: the front bolt struck before

194
00:08:07,055 --> 00:08:08,015
the back bolt!

195
00:08:08,195 --> 00:08:09,425
Who is right?

196
00:08:09,615 --> 00:08:10,765
Both of them.

197
00:08:11,115 --> 00:08:14,795
On a spacetime map where space runs sideways

198
00:08:14,645 --> 00:08:18,645
and time runs upward the platform observer's now

199
00:08:18,495 --> 00:08:21,145
is a flat horizontal slice across space.

200
00:08:21,285 --> 00:08:24,985
But for the moving passenger, that entire slice

201
00:08:24,835 --> 00:08:27,355
of now tilts upward in the direction they

202
00:08:27,205 --> 00:08:27,985
are moving.

203
00:08:28,095 --> 00:08:33,075
Motion literally rotates what counts as the present!

204
00:08:33,784 --> 00:08:37,204
Across a living room, a tilting slice of

205
00:08:37,054 --> 00:08:38,764
now sounds harmless.

206
00:08:38,784 --> 00:08:42,764
At everyday walking speed, the tilt across ten

207
00:08:42,614 --> 00:08:45,634
meters is less than a trillionth of a

208
00:08:45,484 --> 00:08:48,914
millionth of a second far too tiny for

209
00:08:48,764 --> 00:08:50,774
any human to notice.

210
00:08:50,624 --> 00:08:53,564
But a tilted straight line has a sneaky

211
00:08:53,414 --> 00:08:57,614
property: the farther out you extend it, the

212
00:08:57,464 --> 00:09:00,854
higher its far end swings.

213
00:09:00,754 --> 00:09:03,394
So extend that line two and a half

214
00:09:03,244 --> 00:09:08,564
million light-years away, straight across intergalactic space to

215
00:09:08,414 --> 00:09:10,654
the Andromeda Galaxy.

216
00:09:10,504 --> 00:09:15,214
In nineteen eighty-nine, mathematical physicist Roger Penrose used

217
00:09:15,064 --> 00:09:18,494
this exact geometry to reveal one of the

218
00:09:18,344 --> 00:09:23,124
strangest consequences in all of relativity the Andromeda

219
00:09:22,974 --> 00:09:24,124
Paradox.

220
00:09:23,984 --> 00:09:26,734
Imagine two friends passing each other on a

221
00:09:26,584 --> 00:09:30,044
sidewalk here on Earth, walking at a completely

222
00:09:29,894 --> 00:09:33,534
ordinary pace of five kilometers per hour.

223
00:09:33,384 --> 00:09:36,694
One friend is walking away from Andromeda; the

224
00:09:36,544 --> 00:09:38,734
other is walking toward it.

225
00:09:38,584 --> 00:09:41,654
Because Andromeda sits two and a half million

226
00:09:41,504 --> 00:09:47,684
light-years away, that microscopic walking-speed tilt magnifies across

227
00:09:47,534 --> 00:09:49,214
space into days.

228
00:09:49,064 --> 00:09:52,494
For the friend walking away, their tilted slice

229
00:09:52,344 --> 00:09:55,574
of now cuts across Andromeda days before an

230
00:09:55,424 --> 00:09:58,674
alien council even holds a meeting to decide

231
00:09:58,524 --> 00:10:01,614
whether to launch a space fleet toward Earth.

232
00:10:01,464 --> 00:10:04,294
Yet for the friend walking right beside them

233
00:10:04,144 --> 00:10:08,494
literally close enough to shake hands their opposite

234
00:10:08,344 --> 00:10:12,374
tilt slices across Andromeda more than a week

235
00:10:12,224 --> 00:10:14,994
later, days after the alien fleet has already

236
00:10:14,844 --> 00:10:18,134
launched and is streaking across the stars!

237
00:10:17,984 --> 00:10:21,264
Two people on the same sidewalk, at the

238
00:10:21,114 --> 00:10:25,454
exact same moment, disagreeing by eight full days

239
00:10:25,314 --> 00:10:28,004
on whether a cosmic invasion has already begun.

240
00:10:29,172 --> 00:10:29,862
Hold on.

241
00:10:30,012 --> 00:10:32,792
If one friend's now says the fleet already

242
00:10:32,642 --> 00:10:35,542
launched while the other says the vote hasn't

243
00:10:35,542 --> 00:10:39,242
